===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A cry or [[shout]].
#: {{ux|en|He heard a '''call''' from the other side of the room.}}
# The characteristic [[cry]] of a bird or other animal.
#: {{ux|en|That sound is the distinctive '''call''' of the cuckoo bird.}}
# A [[beckon]]ing or [[summon]]ing.
#: {{ux|en|I had to yield to the '''call''' of the wild.}}
#* {{RQ:Spectator|issue=181|date=27 September 1711|page=440|passage=Dependance is a perpetual '''call''' upon humanity, and a greater incitement to tenderness and pity, than any other motive whatsoever.}}
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=V|chapter=XXIII|page=117|passage=But they had hoped that, when peace had been restored, when no '''call''' of duty required him {{q-q|{{w|William III of England}}}} to cross the sea, he would generally, during the summer and autumn, reside in his fair palaces and parks on the banks of the Thames, [...]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|magazine=Latina|year=2007|volume=11|page=101|passage=We actually have a '''call''' tomorrow, which is a Sunday, right after my bridal shower. I have to make enchiladas for 10 people!}}
# A telephone [[conversation]]; a [[phone call]].
#: {{ux|en|I received several phone '''calls''' today.}}
#: {{ux|en|I received several '''calls''' today.}}
# An instance of calling someone on the telephone.
#: {{ux|en|I made a '''call''' to Jim, but he didn’t answer.}}
# A short [[visit]], usually for [[social]] purposes.
#: {{ux|en|I paid a '''call''' to a dear friend of mine.}}
#* {{RQ:Cowper Task|pages=13–14|pageref=13|passage=He [...] ſeldom waits, / Dependent on the baker's punctual '''call''', / To hear his creaking panniers at the door, / Angry and ſad and his laſt cruſt conſumed.}}
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Age of Consent|year=1962|page=149|passage=Podson stayed till after five, though he handsomely apologized for outstaying a '''call'''. "The fact is, I never think of the time, when I get talking to a really intelligent woman...'}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A visit by a ship or boat to a port.
#: {{ux|en|The ship made a '''call''' at Southampton.}}
# A [[decision]] or [[judgement]].
#: {{ux|en|That was a good '''call'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author={{w|Paul Vautin}}|title=Turn It Up!|location=Sydney|publisher=Pan Macmillan Australia |page=2|text=Fair '''call''' actually, because don't the girls blow up when you miss an anniversary?}}
# The right to speak at a given time during a debate or other public event; the [[floor]].
#: {{ux|en|The Prime Minister has the '''call'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|I give the '''call''' to the Manager of Opposition Business.}}
# {{lb|en|finance}} {{ellipsis of|en|call option}}.
# {{lb|en|cricket}} The act of calling to the other batsman.
# {{lb|en|cricket}} The [[state]] of being the batsman whose [[role]] it is to call (depends on where the ball goes.)
